{
  "title": "Mallet Finger",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6014/mallet-finger",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Oct 21 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Mallet Finger is a finger deformity caused by disruption of the terminal extensor tendon distal to DIP joint",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically when the distal phalanx rests at ~45° of flexion with lack of active DIP extension.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually extension splinting of DIP joint for 6-8 weeks. Surgical management is indicated for volar subluxation of the distal phalanx, chronic injuries, or significant arthritis.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Risk factors",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "usually occur in the work environment or during participation in sports",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "common in young to middle-aged males and older females",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most frequently involves long (most common), ring and small fingers of dominant hand",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Zone 1 in the Kleinert and Verdant classification of extensor tendon injuries",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism of injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**traumatic impaction blow**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "usually caused by a traumatic impaction blow (i.e. sudden forced flexion) to the tip of the finger in the extended position.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "forces the **DIP joint into forced flexion**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**dorsal laceration**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "a less common mechanism of injury is a sharp or crushing-type laceration to the dorsal DIP joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
              "header": true,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 3,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "**Doyle's Classification of Mallet Finger Injuries**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Type I",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Closed injury with or without small dorsal avulsion fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/doyle_classification.jpg",
                          "caption": "Doyle Classification of mallet fingers"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Type II",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Open injury (laceration)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/doyle_classification.jpg",
                          "caption": "Doyle Classification of mallet fingers"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Type III",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Open injury (deep soft tissue abrasion involving loss of skin and tendon substance)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/doyle_classification.jpg",
                          "caption": "Doyle Classification of mallet fingers"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Type IV",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Mallet fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A = distal phalanx physeal injury (pediatrics)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "B = fracture fragment involving 20% to 50% of articular surface (adult)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "C = fracture fragment >50% of articular surface (adult)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/doyle_classification.jpg",
                          "caption": "Doyle Classification of mallet fingers"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "primary symptoms",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "painful and swollen DIP joint following impaction injury to finger",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "often in ball sports",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "inspection",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "fingertip rest at ~45° of flexion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "motion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**lack of active DIP extension**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recommended views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "AP/Lateral of the finger",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "often see bony avulsion at the base of the distal phalanx",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/Xray%20-%20lateral_moved.jpg",
                      "caption": "Mallet finger with avulsion fracture"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "may be a ligamentous injury with normal bony anatomy",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/mallet.jpg",
                      "caption": "mallet finger, ligamentous"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "assess for distal phalanx subluxation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ultrasound",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "not typically necessary",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "loss of tendon motion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "complete or partial thickness tears",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "avulsion fracture",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "fluid at the tendon insertion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "extension splinting of DIP joint for 6-8 weeks for 24 hours daily",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6014/images/Splints_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**acute soft tissue injury** (<12 weeks)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**small or minimally displaced bony mallet injury without joint subluxation**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "chronic injury (>12 weeks) with supple, congruent, and nonarthritic joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "maintain free movement of the PIP joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "if in a swan-neck posture at the time of injury, block PIP full extension with dorsal blocking splint",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "3",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "worn for 6-8 weeks",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "typically followed by 4 weeks of nocturnal splinting (controversial)",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "bony injuries splinted until fracture union",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "volar splinting has fewer complications than dorsal splinting",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "avoid hyperextension",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "due to risk of skin necrosis",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "3",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "begin progressive flexion exercises at 6 weeks",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "No differences in patient satisfaction or extensor lag between operative and nonoperative treatment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "~80% of patients have satisfactory outcome",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "CRPP vs ORIF",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**absolute indications**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "**volar subluxation of distal phalanx**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "relative indications",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": ">50% of articular surface involved",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": ">2mm articular gap",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "open injuries",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "surgical repair of the terminal tendon",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "traumatic tendon laceration",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "surgical reconstruction of terminal tendon",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**chronic injury** (> 12 weeks) without contracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "segmental tendon loss",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "tendon reconstruction has a high complication rate (~ 50%)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "DIP arthrodesis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "painful, stiff, arthritic DIP joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Swan neck deformity correction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "Swan neck deformity present",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Techniques",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**CRPP**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "approach",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "percutaneous",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "dorsal extension block pinning with joint pinned in extension",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "Ishiguro technique vs. Modified Ishiguro technique",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**ORIF**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "approach",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "dorsal midline incision",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "simple pin fixation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "dorsal blocking pin",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Surgical reconstruction of terminal tendon",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "repair",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "direct repair often accompanied by trans-articular pin",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "this may be done with direct repair/tendon advancement, tenodermodesis, or spiral oblique retinacular ligament reconstruction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Swan neck deformity correction**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "techniques to correct Swan neck deformity include",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "lateral band tenodesis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "FDS tenodesis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Fowler central slip tenotomy",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "minimal Swan Neck deformities may correct with treatment of the DIP pathology alone",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Extensor lag**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "a slight residual extensor lag of < 10° may be present in up to 40% after either closed or operative treatment, however, no functional deficit.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "lengthening of the terminal tendon by 1mm leads to a 25 degree extensor lag",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Reduced DIP flexion**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "risk for adhesions due to close proximity to the skin and the DIP joint",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "shortening the extensor tendon by 1mm leads to reduced DIP flexion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Swan neck deformities**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs due to",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "retracted terminal insertion leading to proportionally excessive pull on P2 from central slip",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "attenuation of volar plate and transverse retinacular ligament at PIP joint",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "dorsal subluxation of lateral bands",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "resulting PIP hyperextension",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "contracture of triangular ligament maintains deformity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Skin issues (maceration, ulceration, and nail deformities)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs at some degree in about 70% of those treated nonoperatively",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "limited soft tissue coverage puts the wound at risk of dehiscence and infection",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
